    "body": "## Error\n\nDuring view builds, `AlertNavigatorETL` fails and the orchestrator skips it:\n\n```\n[ViewsOrchestrator] Creating all views...\n[AlertNavigatorETL] Starting Alert Navigator view build\n[ViewsOrchestrator] Skipping alert_navigator due to error: [FIELD_NOT_FOUND] No such struct field tenantId in cfg, id, rules.\n[TransactionDetailViewETL] Starting view build\n```\n\n## Root cause\n\nIn `automation-orchestrator/Table_ETLs/alert_navigator.py`, `_build_network_eval` (line 488) projects `t.tenantId` after exploding `net_obj.messages[].typologies[]`:\n\n```python\nF.col(\"t.tenantId\").alias(\"typology_tenant_id\"),\n```\n\nThis assumes each typology entry inside a network_map configuration carries a `tenantId` field. In the actual production data it does not. Inspecting bronze `network_map` confirms this:\n\n- Row count: 3\n- Typologies with `tenantId`: 0\n- Typologies without `tenantId`: 93\n- Every typology struct's keys: `['id', 'cfg', 'rules']`\n\nThe network_map schema places `tenantId` at the top level of the document only — not on messages, not on typologies, not on rules. The bronze layer already extracts it as `tenant_id`. The `t.tenantId` projection therefore references a field that does not exist at that nesting level, and Spark fails with `[FIELD_NOT_FOUND] No such struct field tenantId in cfg, id, rules`, which matches the observed struct exactly.\n\nThe alert payload's typology struct (`alert_data_obj.tadpResult.typologyResult[]`) *does* carry `tenantId`, which is why the same field name works at lines 208 and 363. The two structs were conflated in `_build_network_eval`.\n\n## Proposed solution\n\nRemove the `t.tenantId` projection. The aliased column `typology_tenant_id` is not carried forward in the subsequent `.select(...)` at lines 491–503, so it is dead code — its only effect today is to crash the build.\n\n**File:** `automation-orchestrator/Table_ETLs/alert_navigator.py`\n\n**Change:** Delete line 488:\n\n```python\nF.col(\"t.tenantId\").alias(\"typology_tenant_id\"),\n```\n\nNo further downstream changes are required. If a `typology_tenant_id` column is ever needed by a consumer, it should be derived from the network_map's top-level `tenantId` (already selected as `tenant_id` at line 465), since that is where tenant scoping lives in this data model.\n\nAfter the fix, `alert_navigator` views should build cleanly alongside the other views.",
